21	21	I quote (or hilariously misquote) sacred texts and proverbs in almost every situation	I am a sacred servant of Bahamut	I am bonded to the Goldclaw Clan	I am bad at memorization	Urlingscar "Thunderbelly" Rhogar wasn't always known as such. He was once just another egg among many in the sacred hatchery of the Goldclaw Clan. Yet, from the moment he emerged from his egg, with an unusually loud burp and a belly that shook like a hill giant's laughter, it was clear he was unique.\n\nRhogar was born in the mountainous region of Eregoria, within the sacred caverns of the Goldclaw Clan, known for their golden scales and reverence for the Platinum Dragon, Bahamut. Unlike his fellow Dragonborn, Rhogar was not blessed with the wisdom or intellect typical of his kin. However, what he lacked in smarts, he more than made up for with his boundless charisma and brute strength. Rhogar could make a stone statue laugh and bend an iron bar as though it were a twig.\n\nIt was decided by the clan elders that Rhogar, with his unusual abilities, would be best served in the temple. He was to be an acolyte, a sacred servant of Bahamut, which was an honor, of course. And so, he began his training under the old, patient, and somewhat deaf High Priest, Cindervox.\n\nRhogar embraced his role with enthusiasm. He was just terrible at remembering the holy texts. His misquotes became the stuff of legend within the Goldclaw Clan. "Praise Bahamut, the Platinum Chicken!" he'd shout, or "In the beginning, Bahamut created dragons and humans, and on the second day, he created the all-you-can-eat buffet." He once advised a grieving widow that "Bahamut works in mischievous ways," and frequently invoked "the divine right to party."\n\nHis misquotes were often so hilariously wrong that even the most solemn rituals turned into bouts of laughter. Though some of the elder clergy were initially scandalized, Rhogar's infectious joy, sincerity, and his genuine (though somewhat misguided) devotion to Bahamut couldn't be ignored. They decided that perhaps, in his own way, Thunderbelly was sharing Bahamut's blessings more effectively than any of them ever had.\n\nWhen Rhogar finally left the temple to become a wandering paladin, it was with tears of laughter and heartfelt farewells. Armed with his faith, his mighty strength, and his unending store of hilariously wrong sacred quotes, Urlingscar "Thunderbelly" Rhogar set off to bring the laughter of Bahamut to the rest of the world.\n\nNow he roams from town to town, a beacon of light, laughter, and occasional facepalms. He leaves joy in his wake, and the legend of the Goldclaw Clan's most humorous paladin grows with every hilariously misquoted scripture. It's hard to say what Bahamut makes of his most unique acolyte, but no one can deny that Rhogar's belief is as strong as his biceps, and just as likely to leave a lasting impression.	25	6'8"	300lbs.	Blue	Gold	None	Rhogar spent most of his early life within the protective caverns of the Goldclaw Clan. Raised to be an acolyte of Bahamut, he was groomed into becoming a guardian of the faith. However, his knack for misquoting holy texts made him a local legend. Once he'd learnt all he could (or at least, all he could remember), he decided to take the teachings of Bahamut (or his version of them) out into the wider world.\n\nRhogar carries a tiny sketch portrait of a goblin and an amulet. He forgot how they came into his possession.	Acolyte	Chaotic Good	Urlingscar "Thunderbelly" Rhogar is a towering figure, standing at 6'8" and built like a fortress. His scales gleam like gold coins in sunlight, catching and reflecting light that makes him hard to miss. His eyes, a bright blue, always hold a spark of mischief. There's a large belly that wobbles with laughter, lending him his nickname. He carries a Warhammer, almost as big as a halfling, with a carved laughing dragon on the pommel. His booming laugh and larger-than-life personality make him a memorable figure wherever he goes.	The Goldclaw Clan, especially the high priest Cindervox, are still close allies. They keep in touch by sending letters filled with clan news and correct holy texts (which Rhogar still misquotes). Rhogar also made friends with a group of wandering minstrels who loved his humor and often travel with him. They've formed a group known as "Thunderbelly's Troubadours". He's also loosely associated with the local Paladin Order of the region he's currently in, though their relationship is often strained due to his unorthodox methods.
