19	19	-I have a deep appreciation for the beauty of nature\n\n-I am slow to trust others, but fiercely loyal to those I consider friends.	- Greater Good: My tribe taught me that the strongest should protect the weak, and I will always stand up for those who cannot defend themselves. (Good)\n\n- Independence: I must rely on myself and my own strength to survive in this world. (Chaotic)	- I am searching for a purpose in life beyond the endless cycle of violence and bloodshed.\n\n- My tribe cast me out, but I still feel a deep connection to my people and their way of life.	- I can be quick to anger and slow to forgive.\n\n- I am prone to recklessness, and sometimes act without thinking things through.	Deep in the heart of Mara Cuora, a wandering barbarian roamed the land. His name was Korgoth, and he was born to the Grimmclaw tribe that resided in the mountains of Damabrae. Korgoth was raised in the way of the warrior, and he quickly became one of the most skilled fighters of his tribe. However, as he grew older, Korgoth became disillusioned with the ways of his people.\n\nKorgoth began to question whether this way of life was truly sustainable. He saw how his people were struggling to feed and care for their own, even as they continued to expand their territory and fight for more resources. He began to wonder if there was more to life than simply fighting and killing.\n\nHis doubts only grew when he saw the impact of his tribe's conquests on the surrounding lands. Villages were burned, crops were destroyed, and families were torn apart. Korgoth could not help but wonder if all this violence was really necessary, or if there was a better way to live.\n\nDespite his many victories, he yearned for something deeper, something that would give his life purpose beyond the thrill of combat. Korgoth tried to find this purpose within his tribe, but he was met with nothing but scorn and derision. One day, Korgoth made the difficult decision to leave his tribe and began wandering