41	41	Fang Bo is the embodiment of still water running deep. At first glance, he appears serene, even passive, his voice seldom rising above a gentle murmur. But beneath that calm exterior lies a fiercely observant mind and a spirit brimming with determination. He believes in the principle of action through inaction, often letting situations unfold naturally before intervening. When he speaks, his words are considered and thoughtful, each one carrying the weight of years of contemplation. Despite his peaceful demeanor, when the need arises, Fang Bo’s martial prowess is a sight to behold, his movements a harmonious dance of precision and power.	Balance is the cornerstone of Fang Bo’s belief system. He holds that there is a time for peace and a time for action, and discerning between the two is the key to a fulfilling life. He firmly believes that all beings have the potential for both good and evil, and it is their choices that tip the balance. Upholding justice, for him, isn't about punishing the wicked but about restoring this balance. Knowledge and understanding are, in his eyes, the true paths to enlightenment and the means to navigate the complexities of existence.	The memory of his younger sister is etched deep into Fang Bo's heart. Every action, every decision he takes is in some way a tribute to her, a promise to never let such a tragedy befall someone under his watch. The jade dragon pendant around his neck was a gift from her, and it remains his most cherished possession. Additionally, his bond with the Tianlong Monastery is unbreakable. They were his salvation in his darkest hour, and he would go to great lengths to protect its sanctity and the wisdom it holds.	For all his wisdom, Fang Bo struggles with the shadow of his past. His guilt over his sister's death often clouds his judgment, making him hesitant or overly cautious in situations that remind him of that fateful day. This trauma also makes him wary of forming close attachments, as the fear of losing another loved one looms large. At times, his dedication to balance can also be a hindrance, making him appear indecisive or even apathetic, especially when a swift decision is required.	Fang Bo hails from the distant and secluded Tianlong Monastery, perched atop the Dragon’s Breath Mountain. In this serene environment, he was brought up on tales of ancient warriors and philosophers, surrounded by whispered legends of celestial dragons and the balance of the universe. Tianlong Monastery isn’t just a place of learning; it is a center of spiritual energy, where monks hone not only their physical prowess but their ability to connect deeply with the world and its energies.\n\nIn his youth, Fang Bo was not destined for the monastic life. He was once a proud and brash member of a renowned acrobat troupe, traveling city to city, showcasing daring feats. It was during one of these performances that tragedy struck. A misstep caused a catastrophic fall, taking the life of his younger sister and leaving Fang Bo with a broken spirit. Grief-stricken, he sought solace and found himself at the foot of Dragon’s Breath Mountain. The monks of Tianlong took him in, teaching him the ways of balance, both in the physical world and within his tormented soul. It was there that he transformed, dedicating decades to mastering both martial arts and the philosophy of inner peace.	40	5'7"	150 lbs.	Hazel	Tan	Bald	Unknown to many, Fang Bo is also a guardian of a hidden chamber within the Tianlong Monastery that houses ancient scrolls detailing powerful, long-forgotten martial techniques. Only a select few know of its existence, and even fewer have been deemed worthy of accessing its secrets. In his leisure, Fang Bo indulges in playing the guqin, a classical Chinese stringed instrument, its melodies echoing his own journey of sorrow, redemption, and hope.	Urchin	Chaotic Neutral	Fang Bo stands at a modest 5'7", his bald head reflecting the sun's rays and making him appear almost ethereal in the right light. Years of rigorous training and meditation have given his body a lean, muscular build, and his skin bears an earthy tan from countless hours under the mountain sun. His eyes, deep pools of hazel, hold a calmness that contrasts sharply with the scars that mark his arms and back, mementos of battles past. Often seen wearing loose, ochre robes that billow softly with his every movement, the only hint of opulence about him is a jade pendant shaped like a dragon, always hanging around his neck.	Throughout his journeys, Fang Bo has forged strong bonds, both within and outside the monastery. The monks of Tianlong are his brothers and sisters, a family bound not by blood but by shared purpose. Outside, he has alliances with the enigmatic Order of the Jade Lotus, a group of mystics with whom the monks occasionally collaborate. His reputation as a mediator has also made him a respected figure among various factions, including the Silk Whisper thieves guild, who often seek his counsel in times of internal conflict. In every alliance, Fang Bo remains a symbol of balance and wisdom, always striving for harmony amidst the chaos.
